I would like \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}num}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}5\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}} sandwiches, and my friend would like \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}num}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}5.6e\PYGZhy{}10\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}} drinks. Yes, these are odd choices with a range of \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}numrange}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}5.6e\PYGZhy{}10\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}}5\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}}.

I measured the diffraction angle of the purple line to be \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}ang}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}12;3;45\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}} using the spectrometer. The line was produced by a bulb with a current of \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}qty}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}5(0.1)\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}}\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}A}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}}. I measured over a voltage range of \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}qtyrange}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}1\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}}5\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}}\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}V}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}} with increments of \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}qty}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}1\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}}V\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}}.

The end results found odd results for \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}qtylist}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}1;3;5\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}}\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}V}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}}. This may be because of the definition of the ohm (\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}unit}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}ohm}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}}), particularly in the microohm (\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}unit}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}micro\PYGZbs{}ohm}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}}) range. It could also be because of the limited resolution of the multimeter used below \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}qty}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZob{}}5 \PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}pm} 1\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}\PYGZob{}}\PYG{k}{\PYGZbs{}micro\PYGZbs{}ohm}\PYG{n+nb}{\PYGZcb{}}.
